WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.171
Lesson 2.2: Avatar-Based Performance.

2
00:00:04.171 --> 00:00:07.553
GENAI 101 - Module 2: Theater.

3
00:00:07.553 --> 00:00:14.317
Presented by Idowu David Awoyemi, The University of Alabama.

4
00:00:14.917 --> 00:00:17.695
In this lesson you'll direct an avatar.

5
00:00:17.695 --> 00:00:28.447
You'll take a short monologue, score it into beats, decide
what the performer does in each beat, and build it in an
animation tool one scene at a time.

6
00:00:28.447 --> 00:00:36.280
You'll use the tool's AI voice for a first pass, then record
your own voice for at least one beat and compare.

7
00:00:36.280 --> 00:00:41.692
The finished video, with captions, goes to the gallery with
your beat sheet.

8
00:00:42.292 --> 00:00:51.755
Take any speech and draw a line wherever the speaker's
tactic changes: from pleading to threatening, from
remembering to deciding.

9
00:00:51.755 --> 00:00:56.632
Each segment is a beat, and each beat gets one word for its
tactic.

10
00:00:56.632 --> 00:00:59.834
Directors and actors score scripts this way.

11
00:00:59.834 --> 00:01:06.968
Animators need the same thing, because in an animation tool
nothing happens unless you specify it.

12
00:01:07.568 --> 00:01:12.498
On stage, an actor makes hundreds of micro-decisions
continuously.

13
00:01:12.498 --> 00:01:22.210
In an animation tool, you control four variables per beat:
gesture and posture, facial expression, position and camera,
and voice.

14
00:01:22.210 --> 00:01:26.617
That forces the computational thinking moves into the open.

15
00:01:26.617 --> 00:01:36.105
You decompose the speech, recognize where tactics shift,
abstract each beat into four choices, and sequence them on a
timeline.

16
00:01:36.105 --> 00:01:44.696
Students who struggle to say what an actor 'does' often find
it easier when they have to tell a character to do it.

17
00:01:45.296 --> 00:01:53.409
Tools like Animaker and Synthesia offer AI text-to-speech,
automatic lip-sync, and sometimes gesture suggestions.

18
00:01:53.409 --> 00:02:03.820
They make the avatar look alive with almost no input, and
that's exactly the problem: default delivery is even,
pleasant, and dramatically empty.

19
00:02:03.820 --> 00:02:08.271
That's why you'll record your own voice for at least one
beat.

20
00:02:08.271 --> 00:02:13.656
Listen to the two side by side and write one sentence about
the difference.

21
00:02:13.656 --> 00:02:18.035
Your beat sheet is what makes the performance mean
something.

22
00:02:18.635 --> 00:02:24.431
When you review two classmates, watch with their beat sheet
open beside the video.

23
00:02:24.431 --> 00:02:28.954
Say where the performance matched the plan and where it
drifted.

24
00:02:28.954 --> 00:02:38.637
That's co-review, and it's the collaboration competency in
practice: feedback against a shared plan rather than a shrug
and a compliment.

25
00:02:38.637 --> 00:02:46.270
Use a public-domain text or your own scene from lesson two
point one, keep captions on, and enjoy directing.
